    faqs: [
      { question: 'Where would a Los Alamitos injury case be filed?', answer: 'A lawsuit for injury may be filed in the county where the injury happened or the county where a defendant lives (Code of Civil Procedure section 395(a)). An injury in Los Alamitos can be filed in the Orange County Superior Court, which hears civil cases at the Central Justice Center in Santa Ana; an injury in Long Beach can be filed in the Superior Court of Los Angeles County. Los Alamitos’s criminal and traffic cases are filed at the West Justice Center in Westminster.' },
      { question: 'How do I get the police report for a Los Alamitos crash?', answer: 'It depends on where the crash happened. The Los Alamitos Police Department handles collisions on city streets; traffic collision reports are requested online, and attorneys and insurers need their client’s written authorization. Crashes on the 605 are handled by the California Highway Patrol’s Westminster Area office. Either way, keep the report number you were given at the scene.' },
      { question: 'A dog bit me in Los Alamitos. Who takes the report?', answer: 'Los Alamitos is served by OC Animal Care, so report a bite to OC Animal Care through its online bite reporting form or its rabies control desk. Under California Civil Code section 3342, a dog’s owner is liable for bite injuries suffered in a public place or while lawfully on private property, even if the dog never bit anyone before. Get medical care and the owner’s name and insurance information.' },
      { question: 'I hit my head in a Los Alamitos crash or fall. What should I do?', answer: 'Get medical care first. The CDC lists danger signs that need emergency care right away, such as a headache that gets worse and does not go away or repeated vomiting, and it notes that a mild brain injury or concussion can be present even when tests do not show it. A nearby hospital is UCI Health — Los Alamitos on Katella Avenue. Keep every record, and ask family or coworkers to note changes in memory, mood, or concentration, because a brain injury claim depends on documenting them over time.' },
      { question: 'How long do I have to bring a claim?', answer: 'Generally two years from the injury to file a lawsuit (Code of Civil Procedure section 335.1). If the City of Los Alamitos, the State, or a school district may be responsible — a city street, a freeway, a campus — a written government claim is generally due within six months (Government Code section 911.2). Claims against a doctor or hospital follow a separate rule with its own time limits (Code of Civil Procedure section 340.5).' },
    ],
